    "field": "Ocean biogeochemistry",
    "problem": "Previous studies cannot fully explain a simulated long-term net oxygen gain in a warmer ocean because they overlook an interior-ocean oxygen source generated by an imbalance between enhanced denitrification and slower, incomplete compensation by nitrogen fixation.",
    "method": "This study employs a multi-millennial simulation using a coupled climate-biogeochemistry model under a global-warming scenario to investigate the long-term evolution of the marine oxygen inventory by analyzing the interplay of physical circulation, gas solubility, aerobic respiration, and nitrogen cycle processes like denitrification and fixation.",
    "task": "The task is to explain, using a multi-millennial global warming simulation, why a net loss of fixed nitrogen causes a counterintuitive net gain in the total oceanic oxygen inventory in a warmer future ocean after an initial period of deoxygenation.",
